=: title pico++
=: date 2026-01-09
=: draft true

This rfc outlines the next phase of development for pico. This isn't just a small step improvement over the current gen of services but a reshaping of what we offer. Our demographic is still individual developers but we are also going up-market: targeting teams that are looking to supercharge their dev toolkit with powerful services that get out of their way.

As we have been developing the pico platform, we have creatively avoided offering a critical piece of infrastructure that every individual, team, and company needs: compute. We avoided compute because we have a lot of experience offering compute at our $work. Team pico has years of experience working on a platform as a service, managing thousands of business critical containers and infra. We have managed kubernetes clusters for entire companies including their compute-intensive ml pipelines. We know exactly how much blood, sweat, and tears goes into managing compute for users. It's not easy, being on-call for business critical apps isn't fun, but we think we have something unique to say.

Another reason we were hesitant to offer compute is because of cloud costs. We didn't want to spend months developing pass-through costs or some pricing model that protects us while not gouging our users. If we were to offer compute, it would be self-hosted. Pico++ will not live on hyperscaler cloud infra. We plan on hosting this new service on our own hardware, in a colo. This means the first phase of our compute will have a single location: Michigan. It won't be highly available and we can only offer as much compute as our rack can handle.

Now that the limitations are set and the boundaries are formed, this is what we plan to offer: ephemeral containers. This is our playground, this is where we get to be creative. We aren't stopping at event-based, short-lived containers, that's just the foundation. We plan on offering a CI/CD platform, a cron service, an on-demand web service, and a disappearing code forge.

=> https://pico.prose.sh/rfc-001-radical-experimentation

We have our mission statement, which hasn't changed. Above all else, we are building something that we need ourselves need for our own development. This is the next generation of pico, join us.

# ephemeral containers (pico-compute)

What do ci, faas, crons, batch processing, and preview environments have in common?
	They run compute that has a finite life-cycle
		They start based on an external event
		They shutdown based on a completion event
		They publish their status

Features
	Managed by an ssh cli / tui
	Users deploy containers
	Containers are triggered by external events
		These events can be
			Manually triggered
			Git-based
			Image-based
			Timer-based
			Http-based
	Containers have a max 3 hour lifespan
	When a container completes or the lifespan has been exceeded we spin the container count down to 0
	Multiple containers can be provisioned for the same task, automatically load balanced by us
	Http-based containers will spin up and down based on demand and spun to 0 after an inactive cooldown period
	We will offer linux, mac, and gpu containers

## ssh remote cli

This is our template, before we build a service, we start with how we want it to work.

Command stays alive as long as the user keeps the terminal session alive, max (3 hours)
```
ssh c.pico.sh run -p 80:80 --name web nginx:latest
```

Pipe the image to us
```
docker save web:latest | ssh c.pico.sh run -p 80:80
```

Point your domain to our http load balancer and register an http-based event
```
ssh c.pico.sh sub http.pico.sh run -d --name web web:latest
```

Tail the logs
```
ssh c.pico.sh logs -f web
```

Wait for a git event to build your site
```
ssh c.pico.sh sub git.push.pico run -d -v /artifacts:/app/public --name pico_build pico_build:latest
```

Add a git hook to your repo or manually send the event
```
echo '{"ref": "2f5dc3e", "commit_msg": "chore: update rfc", "ts": 1768270668}' | ssh c.pico.sh pub git.push.pico
```

Run a cron
```
ssh c.pico.sh sub "0 0 * * *" run -d pastes_expired:latest
```

Fetch the build artifacts directly from container
```
rsync -rv c.pico.sh:/pico_build/artifacts ./public/
```

Or inside pico_build make it a multi-stage dockerfile and upload directly to pgs we will automatically inject an SSH key into every docker container that has access to pgs/tuns
```
rsync -rv ./public/ pgs.sh:/pico_build/artifacts
```

Remote into the job
```
ssh c.pico.sh debug pico_build
```
You now have access to:
	A shell with the entire session history
	Dev tooling automatically installed
	Ability install any tools for debugging
	Pressing up-arrow + enter restarts the failed job
The command itself would look something like:
```
docker run --rm -v /artifacts:/app/public --name pico_build pico_build:latest
```

Or drop into the container with a shell to debug
```
docker run --rm -v /artifacts:/app/public --name pico_build pico_build:latest sh
```

Launch a postgres db with a local forward tunnel
```
ssh -L 5432:localhost:5432 c.pico.sh run -e POSTGRES_USER=myuser -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=mypass -e POSTGRES_DB=db postgres:latest
```

Now locally run your client
```
psql -U myuser -d db
```

Or launch the db and then tunnel to it
```
ssh c.pico.sh run -d -e POSTGRES_USER=myuser -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=mypass -e POSTGRES_DB=db --name mydb postgres:latest
ssh -L 5432:localhost:5432 c.pico.sh tun mydb
```

# innovation

When we run a container, we want to enable developers to
	SSH into the system
	Ability to remotely connect their ide or remote-capable dev environment
	Ability to install any tooling they need to debug
	See the failed job at their prompt with session history (pressing up-arrow re-runs the job)
	Have instant access to a code agent for debugging
	Ability to data exfiltrate easily
	The user should not have to install or configure these tools into their containers, it should be automatic

# ci

make it easy to debug a job
	you get access to the shell that is running the job
	you can see session history
	you can install any packages you want
	you can run any commands you want
make it easy to byo development environment into a job
	you can remotely connect vscode or any remote-capable ide into job environment
make it easy to speed up jobs
	most ci platforms help you speed up jobs by running them in parallel
		we want to help speed up test runs by making tests run in parallel
make it easy to self-host runners
make it easy to run jobs as containers
make it easy to run jobs in a pipeline
use pgs for ci artifacts
overal ci job engine paradigm
	sequential jobs happen inside a Dockerfile
		leverage multi-stage dockerfiles where applicable
	parallel jobs happen inside a docker-compose file
	pipelines are a composition of docker-compose files

